The study described examines the prevalence and association of syphilis and chlamydia infections using a national health survey conducted in 2012. It captures data about the population at a single point in time (2012) rather than following individuals over time, which aligns with a **cross-sectional study**. Cross-sectional studies are retained or concurrent at a specific point in time to measure prevalence and observe associations between variables, such as the association found here between chlamydia and syphilis.

Clinical trials (A and C) involve interventions and group comparisons, which are not applicable here. A case-control study (D) starts with disease cases or controls and retrospectively identifies exposures, whereas this study uses a nationally representative sample to analyze concurrent data. The key features of a cross-sectional study (measuring prevalence and associations at one point in time) make **B** the correct answer.

\boxed{B}

