The patient is a 19-year-old female presenting with sudden loss of visual acuity in her right eye. Among the given syndromes, Marfan syndrome (option C) is the most likely diagnosis. Marfan syndrome is a connective tissue disorder characterized by structural abnormalities affecting the eyes, cardiovascular system, and skeletal system. Key eye features of Marfan include progressive myopia, nystagmus, and increased risk of visual acuity reduction. Early-onset Marfan (diagnosed before age 25) is associated with more severe ocular manifestations, including rapid regression of myopia and sudden visual changes. While Ehlers-Danlos syndrome (option A) can cause ocular issues, they are typically less directly linked to sudden vision loss. Sturge-Weber syndrome (option B) is primarily associated with facial lesions and seizures, not acute vision changes. Joint hypermobility syndrome (option D) does not inherently associate with eyeFebruary Acuity (VAC) issues. Thus, Marfan syndrome best aligns with the patient’s symptoms and presentation.

